#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(511.66pt, 788.616pt, "10 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 81.96pt, "diffusion coefficient stands in agreement with the values obtained by means of the simplified, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 98.06pt, "mono-exponential fit, at least in terms of the order of magnitude.  ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 121.94pt, "It is worth noting that the obtained transmission coefficient differs from the experimentally ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 138.26pt, "observed value, ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(151.22pt, 138.26pt, "𝑇∼60")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(187.01pt, 138.26pt, "–")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(193.01pt, 138.26pt, "70%")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(217.13pt, 138.26pt, " [6].This discrepancy may be related to three factors: (1) the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 154.46pt, "membrane has a composite structure, which makes the estimation of the input parameter ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(511.3pt, 154.46pt, "𝑅")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(518.74pt, 156.86pt, "𝑏")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 170.54pt, "value cumbersome; (2) the membrane is porous and therefore optically heterogeneous; and (3) ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 186.5pt, "as shown experimentally, the main pigment undergoes photodegradation. In such a case, the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 202.58pt, "purely diffusive model considered in this work does not capture the full physics of the process. ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 218.54pt, "At the same time, it is worth emphasizing that the pigment diffusion coefficient obtained using ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 234.5pt, "the rigorous Green’s function approach to the signal-phase analysis yields estimates close to ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 250.46pt, "those obtained from the analysis of the signal alone using monoexponential approximations, at ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 266.45pt, "least in terms of the order of magnitude. This agreement is possible due to the relatively high ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 282.77pt, "transmissivity of the system, ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(211.13pt, 282.77pt, "𝑇> 20%")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(258.17pt, 282.77pt, ". ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(88.824pt, 306.77pt, "7.")
  #line-at(106.82pt, 306.77pt, "Summary ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 330.77pt, "The aim of this study was to investigate the possibility of determining pigment diffusion ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 346.73pt, "parameters based on the analysis of phase evolution and the photoacoustic signal of a sample ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 362.69pt, "in response to mass sorption from a semi-infinite medium into a thin membrane. The theoretical ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 378.77pt, "approach relied on the Green's function formalism, assuming a pseudo-stationary heat source ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 394.73pt, "distribution within the sample during a single PA response acquisition. This assumption was ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 410.69pt, "justified by the distinctly different timescales of the mass diffusion process and thermal signal ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 426.65pt, "generation. The model was applied to experimental data obtained from the system analysed in ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 442.63pt, "[6]. Assuming the heat source distribution consistent with the temporal mass distribution as ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 458.71pt, "predicted by the diffusion process in a finite medium, the system's behaviour was examined in ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 474.67pt, "both the linear and nonlinear regimes of the Beer-Lambert law. Step-by-step numerical analysis ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 490.63pt, "of the data enabled determination of the pigment diffusion coefficient in the membrane, as well ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 506.59pt, "as the expected equilibrium transmission coefficient of the system. ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 530.59pt, "The performed simulations demonstrated that the evolution of the signal phase enables precise ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 546.55pt, "determination of transport parameters over a wide range of optical transmissivity of the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 562.51pt, "samples, covering both optically transparent and mixed regimes. The phase variation strongly ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 578.59pt, "depends on the relationship between the thermal properties of the substrate and the sample, and ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 594.55pt, "must be accurately established prior to numerical fitting procedures. However, the results ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 610.51pt, "obtained within the 0")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(176.42pt, 605.95pt, "th")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(182.78pt, 610.51pt, " order approximation, simplifying the theoretical model significantly, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 626.5pt, "also provide reliable accuracy when the sample transmissivity exceeds 20%. The analysis of ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 642.46pt, "the influence of sample transmission on the photoacoustic signal revealed that it is considerably ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 658.54pt, "more susceptible to optical saturation effects. Nevertheless, even in this case, simplified signal ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 674.5pt, "analysis allows for the estimation of transport constants that remain consistent in order of ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 690.46pt, "magnitude with their actual values. ")
]

